---
layout: layouts/base.njk
pagination:
  data: directory.categories
  size: 1
  alias: cat
permalink: "/directory/{{ cat.slug }}/"
eleventyComputed:
  title: "{{ cat.label | safe }} · Largs"
  heading: "{{ cat.label | safe }}"
  subtitle: "{{ cat.blurb | safe }}"
---
{# One page per directory category, same shape as the council register:
   Eleventy renders this template once per entry in directory.categories
   and permalink writes each to its own folder. Real HTML pages —
   bookmarkable, indexable, back-button correct, working with JavaScript
   off. The chips on the front page are plain links to these.

   OPEN CATEGORIES. `open: true` means the category grows as people ask —
   free, alphabetical, no ranking, anyone in the same day their details
   check out. It says nothing about whether the category is empty: a
   category can be open and populated at once, and the page then renders
   the listings first and the "Getting listed" invitation below them.
   Every open page says the list is growing and how to get on it, so
   absence reads as "not sent in yet", never "not recommended". Until
   31 Aug 2026 open meant EMPTY, and commercial categories were held back
   until complete. Dropped: complete is never reachable (shops open and
   close), and holding back the early askers punishes the people who
   engaged. The full rule is (3) in directory.json's _comment.

   EVERY page carries the "who is missing" line, not only the empty ones.
   The populated categories are the likeliest to prompt a correction —
   somebody who runs a group will notice it is absent and say so, which
   is how the gaps get filled. #}

<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="page-title">
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {% if cat.entries | length %}
  {# The blurb is already the page subtitle; repeating it here printed it
     twice on every populated page. #}
  {% if cat.open %}
  <p class="page-intro">Listed in alphabetical order — and still growing as
  people get in touch, so if somewhere's missing it just hasn't been sent in
  yet.</p>
  {% elif cat.order == "curated" %}
  <p class="page-intro">In the order you'd most likely need them.</p>
  {% else %}
  <p class="page-intro">Listed in alphabetical order.</p>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="directory-list">
    {% for item in cat.entries %}
    <div class="directory-item">
      {# LOGO SUPPORT IS A TRIAL, 29 Aug 2026. Delete this block and the
         style at the foot of the file to remove it. The worry: a mark beside
         an entry makes it look weightier than the plain text next to it, so
         businesses with better design outrank those without — ranking by
         budget, on a page that says you cannot buy your way up. #}
      <h2 class="directory-name">
        {% if item.logo %}<img src="{{ item.logo }}" alt="" class="directory-logo" loading="lazy">{% else %}<span class="directory-logo-gap" aria-hidden="true"></span>{% endif %}
        {% if item.url %}<a href="{{ item.url }}" rel="noopener">{{ item.name }}</a>{% else %}{{ item.name }}{% endif %}
      </h2>
      <div class="directory-body">
      {% if item.blurb %}<p>{{ item.blurb }}</p>{% endif %}
      {# One line for how to reach them: where · phone · site. The heading
         is a link too, but a large underlined heading reads as decoration;
         the arrowed link is the affordance the page has already taught.
         It shows the destination rather than the word "website", which
         stays honest when the only site a group has is a Facebook page. #}
      {% if item.where or item.phone %}
      <p class="directory-meta">
        {%- if item.where %}{{ item.where }}{% endif -%}
        {%- if item.where and item.phone %} · {% endif -%}
        {%- if item.phone %}<a href="tel:{{ item.phone | replace(" ", "") }}">{{ item.phone }}</a>{% endif -%}
      </p>
      {% endif %}
      {# A second link, for groups whose Facebook page is where things
         actually happen. The main url stays the one that works without a
         login; this is additive, and the label says what it is so nobody
         clicks into a login wall by surprise. #}
      {% if item.social %}
      <p class="directory-social"><a href="{{ item.social.url }}" rel="noopener">{{ item.social.label }} →</a></p>
      {% endif %}
      {% if item.note %}<p class="directory-note">{{ item.note }}</p>{% endif %}
      </div>
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% else %}
  <p class="page-intro">Nothing here yet. The list will grow, in alphabetical
  order, as people get in touch.</p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if cat.open %}
  {% if cat.entries | length %}<div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>{% endif %}
  <h2>Getting listed</h2>
  <p>It's free — just drop us an email with the details below and we'll get
  your listing up as soon as we can.</p>
  <p><a href="mailto:hello@largs.scot?subject=Directory%20listing">hello@largs.scot</a></p>
  {# Per-category, because the fields genuinely differ. Most of all for
     trades: a sole trader works from home, and "where you are" invites a
     home address onto a public page. Ask for the area covered instead, and
     say we will not publish the address, so nobody has to wonder. #}
  <ul>
    {% for ask in cat.asks %}<li>{{ ask }}</li>
    {% else %}
    <li>Your name</li>
    <li>What you do, in a sentence</li>
    <li>Where you are, or the area you cover</li>
    <li>A phone number, a website, or both</li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
  {# The logo spec that matters is CONTENT, not pixels: a file carrying its
     own wordmark prints the business name twice — once in the artwork, once
     as the heading beside it — and shrinks the mark to make room. Learned by
     looking, 29 Aug 2026. Kept to two sentences so it reads as help rather
     than a specification. #}
  <p class="logo-note">If you're sending a logo, a square image works best —
  just the symbol, not the version with your name in it, since your name is
  already the heading. Anything from 256 pixels up is plenty and we'll crop it
  to fit. No logo is no problem.</p>
  <p>We'll check it's all current before it goes up, and we won't publish
  anything else about you.</p>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <h2>Who have we missed?</h2>
  <p>If that's your logo above and you'd rather we didn't use it, just say and
  we'll take it off, no questions.</p>

  <p>This list is put together by hand, so there'll be gaps. If we've missed
  you, or something's moved, closed or changed, let us know at
  <a href="mailto:hello@largs.scot?subject=Directory%20—%20{{ cat.label }}">hello@largs.scot</a>
  — we'd be glad to hear.</p>
</section>

{# TRIAL STYLING for the logo experiment — scoped here rather than site.css
   so it lifts out with the block above if the answer is no. #}
<style>
  .directory-name{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px}
  /* Square, like an app icon. Fixed box so every mark gets the same room,
     and the spec that matters is CONTENT not size: the mark only, cropped
     close, no wordmark. The business name is already the heading beside it,
     so a logo carrying its own name renders that name twice and shrinks the
     mark to fit. */
  .directory-list{--logo:80px}
  .directory-logo{width:var(--logo);height:var(--logo);object-fit:contain;flex:none}
  .directory-logo-gap{width:var(--logo);height:var(--logo);flex:none}
  /* Body text lines up under the name rather than the page margin, so
     each entry reads as one block instead of an L shape: logo box
     + the 12px flex gap, from the one --logo value above. */
  .directory-body{margin-left:calc(var(--logo) + 12px)}
  .directory-social{margin-top:3px}
  .directory-social a{font-size:.94rem}
  @media (max-width:600px){
    .directory-list{--logo:56px}
  }
</style>
